WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.980
DALE MARKOWITZ: Father's
Day is about a week away,

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.210
and like a lot of
people, I'm not

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.040
going to be able to
see my dad this year.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.372
He's in New Jersey,
and I'm in Texas.

00:00:07.372 --> 00:00:09.330
So I decided I would try
to build him something

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.820
that's easy to share digitally.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.680
A machine learning-powered
searchable video archive.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.930
And at the end of this
episode, I will share with him,

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:19.500
and we'll see what
he thinks of it.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.340
[SPINNING REELS]

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:26.630
My dad has definitely always
been the primary video man.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:29.280
My dad's first video was
the day that I was born.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.700
So he has his camera
on me in the bassinet,

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.490
and I'm looking
very red and gooey.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:38.335
And then my mom has
just given birth.

00:00:38.335 --> 00:00:39.710
And it's amazing
that she allowed

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:40.970
herself to be on camera.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.420
And since then, he's just been
taking tons and tons of video.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.230
So a couple of years ago, my
dad uploaded all of our family

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.820
footage to Google Drive.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.417
So I decided to build my dad
an AI-powered video archive,

00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:56.000
which would allow us to actually
search through all the videos

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.300
and find the most
interesting bits.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.798
Here's the searchable
video archive

00:00:59.798 --> 00:01:01.340
that I ended up
building, that allows

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.890
you to search text and
transcripts and objects

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.810
by term.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.863
Let's take a look
at how I built it.

00:01:08.863 --> 00:01:10.280
The first step of
this project was

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.770
to transfer all of my dad's
videos from Google Drive

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:14.090
to cloud storage.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.570
This way they'd be accessible
to the rest of my GCP project.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:19.350
If you're using files
on your desktop,

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:21.960
you can just upload them
directly from within the UI.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.380
But if you're transferring
files from Google Drive,

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.060
check out my blog to
see the hack I used

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.800
to make uploading much faster.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.720
Next, I analyzed
all those videos

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.670
using the Video
Intelligence API,

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:35.060
which is the main AI workhorse
at the center of this project.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.590
This tool takes
a video as input,

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.690
and it spits out lots of
different annotations,

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.330
like what's actually
going on in an image.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:42.230
Is it a wedding?

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.280
Is it fireworks?

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:43.990
A cute dog?

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:44.490
[BARK]

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.880
It uses computer vision to
detect objects and events

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:47.870
in videos.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.330
It also transcribes the
audio track from videos

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.670
so that we can later
search by what people say.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.710
This is how I was able to
search for my very first steps.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.885
DALE'S DAD: That's Dale walking.

00:01:57.885 --> 00:01:59.690
This is the first
time she's taken

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:01.310
major steps on this floor.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.630
DALE MARKOWITZ: It can also
grab text shown on screen,

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.230
so you can search for things
written on T-shirts or posters

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:07.490
or on signs.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:08.960
Or on birthday cakes--

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.173
this is how I found
my first birthday.

00:02:11.173 --> 00:02:13.340
I also used this feature
to grab the timestamps that

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.100
appeared on screen in some
of my dad's older videos.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.410
Once I extracted all of
these searchable features,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:21.170
I could use them to quickly
search through those videos.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.180
Now, using the Video
Intelligence API

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.720
is pretty straightforward.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.690
You just import the Video
Intelligence library

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.790
and call the Analyze
Video function,

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.910
passing a path to the location
of your stored video in cloud

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:32.720
storage.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.680
Now, because my video
files were massive,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.260
I tried to do all
of this processing

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.510
exclusively in the cloud,
because downloading things

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.030
onto my desktop would
have been really slow.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.010
I chose an architecture
that looked like this.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:44.660
First, like I
showed you earlier,

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.030
I uploaded my videos to a
cloud storage bucket, which

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:48.530
is like a folder in the cloud.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.830
I used uploading a video to
trigger a Cloud Function.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.770
In that cloud function, I called
the Video Intelligence API,

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.540
which analyzed
the uploaded video

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.430
and wrote the data to Firestore.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.170
Finally, I needed a
way to quickly sort

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:01.710
through all of this data.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.430
So for this, I used a
handy non-Google tool built

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.470
by a company called Algolia.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.480
It's a search tool that
lets you upload JSON,

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.970
and then offers an API to
search through it quickly.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:12.920
It handles things like
correcting for typos

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.300
and finding the most
relevant results.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.510
So that was pretty much
how I built the back end.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.610
To build a front end
or our user interface,

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.250
I decided to learn how to use
Flutter, which is a Google

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.020
framework for building apps.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.570
And I built a Flutter web
app, which looks like this.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:27.950
Of course, there
are tons of ways

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:30.033
you could build a front
end without Flutter, using

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:33.560
JavaScript or for IOS
or Android, your choice.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:34.727
And that's it in a nutshell.

00:03:34.727 --> 00:03:36.935
If you want to learn more
about the technical details

00:03:36.935 --> 00:03:38.530
of this project or
to see the code,

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:41.120
make sure you check out my blog
post linked in the description

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:41.820
below.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:42.320
All right.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.690
I guess it's time to show
this app to a real user.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.070
Let's show it to my dad.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:50.450
So, Dad, I built something
for you for Father's Day,

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:53.930
and I'm really excited and also
really nervous to show you.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:54.680
DALE'S DAD: Uh-oh.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.140
DALE MARKOWITZ: It's probably
the most high-effort Father's

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.945
Day gift I've ever worked on.

00:03:58.945 --> 00:04:00.320
DALE'S DAD: Oh,
look at all this.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.120
I see Scooter and
Monticello, so I think

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.760
I know where this is going.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.530
Oh, wow, look at all this.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.380
Wow.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.010
I know all these pictures.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.280
DALE MARKOWITZ: So I took
all of your video in Drive,

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.360
and I analyzed it with machine
learning so that, first of all,

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.550
I split all of your
really long two-hour clips

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.130
into little chunks, and
I made them searchable.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:25.800
So in addition to these ones,
Father's Day and Performance,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.508
you can search by
anything that you want.

00:04:27.508 --> 00:04:28.800
DALE'S DAD: That's interesting.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.280
So can I search for, let's
say, we used to play ping pong.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.830
You think the ping pong
table might show up?

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.038
DALE MARKOWITZ: Yeah, try it.

00:04:39.752 --> 00:04:40.960
DALE'S DAD: Oh, look at that!

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.640
There it is.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:43.620
I remember when I--

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.720
when we bought your first
bicycle, how excited you were.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:56.160
So how about-- wait, this--

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.028
look it here.

00:04:57.028 --> 00:04:58.320
This is what I was looking for.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.180
This video right here.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.910
But it brought all these
other bicycles in, as well.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:05.910
How neat.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.950
Every one of these pictures
has the bike in it.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.440
But this is the specific
one that I was looking at.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.800
So, yeah, I would have
definitely found it.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:14.340
Very neat.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.340
So you really could be very
loose with your search terms

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:18.650
here.

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.987
You know, I guess the looser
it is, the more likely

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:23.070
you are to find it, and
then you can just zoom in.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.960
I guess you can get specific,
if there's a lot of occurrences.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:26.860
This is very nice.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.810
Thank you very much for the
early Father's Day gift.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.007
I can't wait to show Mom.

00:05:32.007 --> 00:05:33.090
DALE MARKOWITZ: Of course.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:34.423
That's all I wanted to show you.

00:05:34.423 --> 00:05:35.775
Thanks for filming with us.

00:05:35.775 --> 00:05:36.400
DALE'S DAD: OK.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.862
So how do you need
me to end this?

00:05:38.862 --> 00:05:41.880
DALE MARKOWITZ: Oh, I have
an idea, I have an idea.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.112
Can you search for Fade Out?

00:05:45.112 --> 00:05:47.070
DALE'S DAD: Oh, yeah, I
said that all the time.

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:54.280
I think at the end of
every video we said that.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:55.810
Let's see, if I--

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.350
let's see how long this one is.

00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:59.220
OK, I'm going to
fade out, pussycat.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.510
DALE MARKOWITZ: Can
I go on the bike?

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:02.545
DALE'S DAD: We're not.

00:06:02.545 --> 00:06:03.628
DALE MARKOWITZ: We're not.

00:06:03.628 --> 00:06:06.470
DALE'S DAD: You sure?

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.500
You sure we're not
going on the bike?

